{#
  A case study's Case Content matrix, block by block.

  Expects: blocks — the caseContent entries, in author order.
  Optional: anchoredHtml — { blockId: html } for content blocks whose h2s have been
            given ids (see the note view); a block not in it renders its own field.

  All the block types are built out: largeImage, legacyImage, twoUpImage, threeUpImage,
  video, lottie, caseTestimonial, contentMethod, code and content. An unknown type falls through and renders nothing rather than erroring
  — which is how caseTestimonial went missing for a while: the block was in the field,
  no branch matched it, and it simply vanished from the page it was placed on.

  Consecutive images run as one block with no timeline connector between them — the
  spine only reappears where the KIND of content changes (see _case-content.scss). A
  connector between every image would chop a sequence into unrelated beats. A video
  counts as imagery for that: it's a <figure> like the rest, so it inherits the same
  spacing and spine rules without any of them naming it.

  Images go through the media.picture macro, which emits a WebP srcset with a fallback
  and puts width/height on the <img> so nothing shifts as it loads.
#}
{% import '_macros/media.twig' as media %}

{% set blocks = blocks ?? [] %}

{# True until the first block has rendered. The block at the top of the page is the
   only one that can be in the first viewport, so it's the only one that ships eager
   with a high fetch priority — everything below stays lazy. Cleared by ANY block,
   not just an image: a study that opens with prose or a video has its first image
   somewhere down the page, where promoting it would take priority from the fonts
   and CSS that decide first paint.

   A <link rel="preload"> would be the wrong tool here — the <picture> below carries
   AVIF, WebP and a fallback behind calc() sizes, and a preload that doesn't match
   the source the browser picks is a second download rather than a saved one. #}
{% set leading = true %}

{% if blocks|length %}
    <div class="c-case-content">
        {% for block in blocks %}
            {% if block.type.handle == 'largeImage' %}
                {% set image = block.largeImage.one() %}
                {% if image %}
                    {# `sizes` describes the slot, and the slot is the full content box
                       — the viewport less the slab inset (2 × rhythm 500) and
                       .c-content's own inline padding, which steps up at md and lg.
                       Getting this wrong is what makes a browser fetch a 3200px file
                       for a 500px slot, or a 640px one for a 1600px slot.

                       Candidates run to 3200 for retina. `w` descriptors already
                       account for device pixel ratio — the browser multiplies `sizes`
                       by the DPR itself — so there's no `2x` to declare, only a large
                       enough candidate to pick. The slot maxes at ~1600 on the xxl
                       design canvas, so 3200 is that at 2×; source images should be
                       3200×1800 for a 1600×900 display size.

                       Craft won't upscale (upscaleImages is off in general.php), so a
                       candidate wider than the source just resolves to the source. A
                       1920px original tops out there — no error, no giant file, just
                       no retina above 1.2×.

                       quality 90 is above the site default of 82: on a case study the
                       image IS the work being shown, and it's displayed large, where
                       compression artefacts in flat colour and type would be obvious.

                       `tone: true` opts into the colour placeholder, which is off
                       everywhere else on the site. These pages are the case for it:
                       full-bleed work the visitor is scrolling through to look at,
                       one image at a time, where a photo arriving on a card of its
                       own colours reads as part of the work. In a Jonson answer the
                       same effect fires next to running text and just flickers.

                       `sizes` is in px, not rem, on purpose: the preload scanner
                       resolves rem in a `sizes` attribute against the browser's
                       initial 16px, not this site's 10px root, so `12.6rem` would
                       declare a 201px inset for a 126px one. The 1600px `min()` is
                       the content column's cap. Same numbers in the pair and trio
                       strings below.

                       (All of this lives OUTSIDE the options hash below — a Twig
                       comment inside a hash literal is a parse error that takes the
                       whole page down with it.) #}
                    <figure class="c-case-content__figure">
                        {{ media.picture(
                            image,
                            '(min-width: 62em) min(100vw - 126px, 1600px), (min-width: 48em) calc(100vw - 81px), calc(100vw - 68px)',
                            {
                                widths: [480, 640, 960, 1280, 1600, 2000, 2400, 3200],
                                fallback: 960,
                                quality: 90,
                                tone: true,
                                loading: leading ? 'eager' : 'lazy',
                                fetchpriority: leading ? 'high' : null,
                            }
                        ) }}
                    </figure>
                    {% set leading = false %}
                {% endif %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'legacyImage' %}
                {# An older, smaller picture — a photo from a note, not a 3200px piece
                   of work. The picture ALWAYS sits on the reading measure with the
                   prose, at its own size when it's narrower than that, never
                   stretched: the ladder is cut to the widths the source actually
                   has, so nothing is upscaled into softness.

                   Has breakout? doesn't enlarge it. It puts a second layer BEHIND
                   it — the same picture, small and blurred, filling the large
                   image's full width — so a modest photo can still hold a full-width
                   beat without being blown up. The sharp copy and its caption sit
                   on top, on the measure. Same tone placeholder and eager/lazy rule
                   as the other imagery; the blurred copy is a small lazy WebP, since
                   nothing sharp survives the blur. #}
                {% set image = block.legacyImage.one() %}
                {% if image %}
                    {% set breakout = block.hasBreakout ?? false %}
                    {% set legacySizes = '(min-width: 48em) min(740px, 100vw - 81px), calc(100vw - 68px)' %}
                    {% set legacyLadder = [480, 740, 1480]|filter(w => w <= (image.width ?? 0)) %}
                    {% set legacyLadder = legacyLadder|length ? legacyLadder : [image.width] %}
                    {% set legacyFallback = min(740, image.width) %}
                    {% set caption = (block.caption ?? '')|trim %}
                    {% set legacyPicture %}
                        {{ media.picture(image, legacySizes, {
                            widths: legacyLadder,
                            fallback: legacyFallback,
                            quality: 90,
                            tone: true,
                            loading: leading ? 'eager' : 'lazy',
                            fetchpriority: leading ? 'high' : null,
                        }) }}
                    {% endset %}
                    {% set legacyCaption %}
                        {% if caption %}
                            <figcaption class="c-case-content__caption">{{ caption }}</figcaption>
                        {% endif %}
                    {% endset %}
                    {# The caption is a direct child of the figure either way, BELOW
                       the blurred box on the breakout — on the page, never over the
                       blur — so the same :has() rule strips the figure's own box and
                       the caption sits on the cream under the picture in both cases. #}
                    {% if breakout %}
                        <figure class="c-case-content__figure c-case-content__figure--backdrop">
                            <div class="c-case-content__backdrop-box">
                                <div class="c-case-content__backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
                                    <img src="{{ image.getUrl({ width: min(640, image.width), format: 'webp', quality: 60 }) }}"
                                        alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                                </div>
                                <div class="c-case-content__figure-inner">{{ legacyPicture }}</div>
                            </div>
                            {{ legacyCaption }}
                        </figure>
                    {% else %}
                        <figure class="c-case-content__figure c-case-content__figure--inline">{{ legacyPicture }}{{ legacyCaption }}</figure>
                    {% endif %}
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'twoUpImage' %}
                {% set pair = [block.twoUpImagePrimary.one(), block.twoUpImageSecondary.one()]|filter(a => a) %}
                {% if pair|length %}
                    {# HALF the slot from md up, where the pair sits side by side, less
                       half the gap each. Below md they stack full width.

                       That assumes the source is cut for this slot — 1600 × 1800, for
                       a 789 × 900 cell at 2×. Feed it a wide landscape image instead
                       and `cover` will scale it up until its height fits before
                       cropping, which needs roughly double these widths; `sizes` only
                       describes layout width, so the browser can't know that and the
                       image comes back soft. Shoot the pair to shape.

                       One image on its own still takes a half cell, so a part-filled
                       pair doesn't silently become a full-width block. #}
                    <div class="c-case-content__pair">
                        {% for image in pair %}
                            <figure class="c-case-content__figure">
                                {{ media.picture(
                                    image,
                                    '(min-width: 62em) calc((min(100vw - 126px, 1600px) - 22px) / 2), (min-width: 48em) calc((100vw - 81px - 22px) / 2), calc(100vw - 68px)',
                                    {
                                        widths: [480, 640, 800, 1024, 1280, 1600],
                                        fallback: 800,
                                        quality: 90,
                                        tone: true,
                                        loading: leading ? 'eager' : 'lazy',
                                        fetchpriority: leading ? 'high' : null,
                                    }
                                ) }}
                            </figure>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                    {% set leading = false %}
                {% endif %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'threeUpImage' %}
                {# Three separate single-asset fields, the same shape the pair uses —
                   left, middle and right as named slots rather than one ordered list.

                   NOT one field placed three times, which is where this started: Craft
                   keys relational data on fieldId alone, with no column naming the
                   instance, so three instances of the same field all wrote into a
                   single pool and only the first read back.

                   Filtered, so a part-filled trio renders what it has rather than
                   holding an empty cell — the same courtesy the pair extends. #}
                {% set trio = [
                    block.threeUpImagePrimary.one(),
                    block.threeUpImageSecondary.one(),
                    block.threeUpImageTertiary.one()
                ]|filter(a => a) %}
                {% if trio|length %}
                    {# A THIRD of the slot from md up, less two gaps rather than the
                       pair's one — three columns have two gutters between them, and
                       subtracting one is the mistake that makes every image in the row
                       come back a third too small.

                       Cells are about 519 at the design canvas (1600 less 2 x 22),
                       so 1040 covers them at 2x. The ladder still runs to 1600 because
                       the WIDEST case is not the desktop cell at all — it is a phone,
                       where these stack full width and a 390pt screen at 3x wants
                       ~1170. Sizing the ladder to the desktop grid is what leaves
                       mobile soft.

                       No aspect-ratio on the row, unlike the pair. Held to the pair's
                       16/9 a cell would be 519 x 900 — a 0.58 portrait — and `cover`
                       would have to scale a landscape source to ~1600 wide before it
                       was tall enough to crop, needing roughly three times the pixels
                       `sizes` describes. Width-only transforms keep each image's own
                       ratio instead, which is also the right call for logos, where a
                       crop is never what you want. The cost is that three images of
                       different shapes leave a ragged bottom edge — shoot the set to
                       one shape. #}
                    <div class="c-case-content__trio">
                        {% for image in trio %}
                            <figure class="c-case-content__figure">
                                {{ media.picture(
                                    image,
                                    '(min-width: 62em) calc((min(100vw - 126px, 1600px) - 44px) / 3), (min-width: 48em) calc((100vw - 81px - 44px) / 3), calc(100vw - 68px)',
                                    {
                                        widths: [320, 480, 640, 800, 1040, 1280, 1600],
                                        fallback: 520,
                                        quality: 90,
                                        tone: true,
                                    }
                                ) }}
                            </figure>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                    {% set leading = false %}
                {% endif %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'video' %}
                {# Self-hosted clip, with the block's Large Image as its poster — the
                   same field the largeImage block uses, so a poster is prepared and
                   sized like any other full-width image.

                   It plays on loop while it's on screen — but there's no `autoplay`
                   attribute, deliberately. That would start the download at page load
                   wherever the block sits, and this one is most of a page down. The
                   markup ships inert and video-autoplay.js starts it on view, which
                   also leaves a sane no-JS state: a poster with controls.

                   `preload="none"` for the same reason — tens of megabytes (Urban's is
                   19MB), and even `metadata` opens the connection and pulls the file's
                   head on every page view.

                   `muted` is not a style choice: browsers refuse autoplay with sound.
                   (These clips have no audio track anyway.)

                   `controls` IS in the markup, and video-autoplay.js takes it away —
                   the same direction as Read more, and for the same reason. Shipped
                   without them, a visitor with no JS gets a poster and no way to play
                   it. Shipped with them, the fallback is a working player, and the
                   only browsers that keep it are the ones that were never going to
                   autoplay: reduced motion, or an autoplay refusal (Safari's Low
                   Power Mode blocks even a silent clip). Where the loop does run, a
                   silent clip playing in place reads as a moving image and has
                   nothing to operate, so the controls go.

                   `playsinline` keeps iOS from taking the video fullscreen on play.

                   width/height come from the POSTER, not the video — Craft records no
                   dimensions for a video asset. Browsers derive the aspect ratio from
                   these exactly as they do on an <img>, so the box is reserved before
                   anything loads. Without a poster there's nothing to derive it from,
                   and the CSS falls back to the page's 16/9 (see _case-content.scss). #}
                {% set clip = block.video.one() %}
                {% set poster = block.largeImage.one() %}
                {% if clip %}
                    <figure class="c-case-content__figure">
                        <video
                            class="c-case-content__video"
                            data-autoplay-video
                            muted
                            loop
                            controls
                            playsinline
                            preload="none"
                            {%- if poster %}
                                poster="{{ poster.getUrl({ width: 1600, format: 'webp', quality: 90 }) }}"
                                width="{{ poster.width }}"
                                height="{{ poster.height }}"
                            {%- endif %}>
                            <source src="{{ clip.url }}" type="{{ clip.mimeType }}">
                        </video>
                    </figure>
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'lottie' %}
                {# A Lottie animation in a full-width figure, played by lottie-block.js
                   while it's on screen and paused when it isn't — the same rhythm as
                   the video block. The markup ships as an empty box: the player and
                   the JSON are only fetched when the block nears the viewport, so a
                   page that has one costs nothing extra above the fold.

                   The figure is the same panel as a full-width image: the column's
                   width at the page's 16/9. The animation plays INSIDE it at the
                   size it was made, centred — a composition scaled up goes soft at
                   its embedded images and type — and only ever scales down, on a
                   panel smaller than it. Its size is read from the JSON once and
                   cached (see lottieSize()) and handed to the CSS as custom
                   properties; without them the animation fills the panel.

                   Background Colour is the ground the animation plays on: a Lottie
                   composition is normally transparent, so without one it would sit on
                   the figure's tan slab. Set inline on the figure, so the loading
                   state is already the right colour.

                   Loop Animation? decides what happens at the end: on, it cycles
                   while on screen; off, it plays through once each time it comes
                   into view and holds its last frame.

                   The asset's Alt Text makes it an image to a screen reader; with
                   none it's decorative and hidden from one — the animation itself
                   carries no text. #}
                {% set anim = block.lottieJson.one() %}
                {% if anim %}
                    {% set size = craft.frontend.lottieSize(anim) %}
                    {% set bg = block.backgroundColour.hex ?? null %}
                    <figure class="c-case-content__figure"{% if bg %} style="background-color: {{ bg }}"{% endif %}>
                        <div
                            class="c-case-content__lottie"
                            data-lottie="{{ anim.url }}"
                            {%- if block.hasLoopedAnimation %} data-lottie-loop{% endif %}
                            {%- if size %} style="--lottie-w: {{ size.w }}px; --lottie-h: {{ size.h }}px"{% endif %}
                            {%- if anim.alt %} role="img" aria-label="{{ anim.alt }}"{% else %} aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}></div>
                    </figure>
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'caseTestimonial' %}
                {# A quote placed in the flow, where the author put it, rather than in
                   the slider at the foot of the page. Same component either way, so it
                   looks the same wherever it's met, and the same field mapping the page
                   template uses — company through craft.frontend.testimonialCompany, so
                   a client can't be named one way here and another below.

                   The page template drops any testimonial placed here from that bottom
                   slider; without that the same quote appears twice on the page. #}
                {% set quoted = block.testimonial.one() %}
                {% if quoted and (quoted.blockquote ?? '') %}
                    <div class="c-case-content__quote">
                        {% include '_components/testimonials' with { items: [{
                            quote: quoted.blockquote,
                            name: quoted.personName ?? '',
                            role: quoted.jobTitle ?? '',
                            company: craft.frontend.testimonialCompany(quoted),
                            image: quoted.profileImage.one() ?? null,
                        }] } only %}
                    </div>
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'contentMethod' %}
                {# "How I can help" — the method timeline, placed in the flow where the
                   author put it. The block carries no fields: the phases are the
                   methodology single's, the same ones a Jonson answer shows for
                   [[method]], so the timeline reads identically wherever it's met.
                   Light dress for the cream slab. The outer box is the container the
                   timeline measures itself against, the inner is the reading measure
                   the phase text lines up on — see .c-case-content__method. #}
                {% set method = craft.frontend.methodology() %}
                {% if method %}
                    <div class="c-case-content__method">
                        <div class="c-case-content__method-inner">
                            {% include '_components/method' with { method: method, light: true } only %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'code' %}
                {# A code snippet — `codeSnippet` is the source (plain text,
                   multiline), `codeLanguage` the dropdown naming it. Rendered by
                   _components/code-block, on the reading measure, like prose. #}
                <div class="c-case-content__code">
                    {% include '_components/code-block' with {
                        code: block.codeSnippet ?? '',
                        lang: block.codeLanguage ?? '',
                        lineNumbers: block.hasLineNumbers ?? false,
                    } only %}
                </div>
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'content' %}
                {# A caller that built an "on this page" nav passes the block's HTML
                   back with ids on its h2s (anchoredHtml, keyed by block id — see
                   craft.frontend.headingAnchors); otherwise the field's own. #}
                {% set prose = craft.frontend.richText((anchoredHtml ?? {})[block.id ?? ''] ?? (block.content ?? '')) %}
                {% if prose %}
                    <div class="c-case-content__text">{{ prose|raw }}</div>
                {% endif %}
                {% set leading = false %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
{% endif %}
